A||N||3||mad dog||Oil Sending Unit||||1||
Z||000000||mad dog||06-19-2000||02:45 PM||||I need to replace my oil sending unit on an 86 SE V-6.  What is the proper way to do it, what tools do I need--basically, can I do it myself?  How long should it take me to replace it?  Thanks for any info/suggestions!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||06-19-2000||02:57 PM||||One of the repairs I did myself! If you have the "right" tools, its a 10 minute job.<P>The right tool was a "stubby" crescent wrench that fits in so you can access the bolts (or nut) that hold it in place, without removing other things to get to the access.  I don't remember if it was a 5/8 or 7/8.  I ended up buying a set of stubby box end wrenches from Mr. Sears.  The oil sending replacement was the only specific use I've had for those wrenches.  More tools for the tool box.  There was an article in a past issue of Tri State Fiero magazine (I think) a couple of years ago, that explained the process.  The sending unit I bought from Associated Auto (S.J. Wynman) as he was the author of the article.||||reg||4
Z||000002||StuGood||06-19-2000||03:18 PM||||Disconnect & remove battery.  Remove plastic battery box sidewall (2 bolts - 7mm socket).<P>Remove connector from sending unit.  It has a big plastic ring lock on it.  Squeeze the ring to distort/unlock it - then pull the connector off the sending unit.  Don't force it -if it doesn't unlock first time, squeeze ring at new (angular) location and try again until it works.<P>Get a 9/16" open end wrench, and unscrew the sending unit.  There's a 9/16" hex on the sending unit, underneath the big black part.<P>Screw new sending unit into the fitting.  Tighten so it won't leak.  Look at ring lock on connector.  Squeeze ring to see how it works.  Simple, huh?<P>Feel stupid that you spent hours trying to figure out how to get that stupid connector off (Oops - that was MY method.  You can skip this step)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">.<P>Note location of the three terminals in the connector, and plug it into the new sending unit.  Reinstall battery.  Start car and verify that gage works.  Reinstall battery box sidewall.<P>Feel good about what you just accomplished (definitely DO this step)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>    ||||reg||14
Z||000003||mad dog||06-20-2000||12:26 PM||||Thanks for the info--sounds like I can do this one!  ||||reg||14
